A simple way to think about it is:

Git World (Databricks, VS Code, GitHub, etc.)

A branch is just a different version of files.

Repository
   ├── main
   ├── feature-a
   └── feature-b


When you create a branch:

main → feature-a


you don't create a new environment.

You simply tell Git:

"I want another version of these files."

The same IDE can switch between branches:

VS Code
   ↓
main

VS Code
   ↓
feature-a


Nothing else changes.

Fabric World

A Fabric workspace is not just files.

It contains:

Reports
Semantic models
Pipelines
Notebooks
Lakehouses
Connections
Permissions
Runtime state

Think of it as a mini application environment.

Workspace
   ├── Files
   ├── Metadata
   ├── Security
   ├── Connections
   └── Running Assets


Because of that, Fabric can't always treat a branch as "just another folder of files."

Git Logic
Branch
    =
Another copy of code

flowchart LR

A[main]

A --> B[feature-a]

A --> C[feature-b]


Very cheap.

Very lightweight.

Fabric Logic

Fabric asks:

"If two developers are changing the same workspace, how do we keep them from impacting each other?"

Their answer was:

Branch
      +
Workspace Isolation

flowchart LR

A[Main Workspace]

A --> B[Feature Workspace A]

A --> C[Feature Workspace B]

B --> D[feature-a]

C --> E[feature-b]

Why Branch Out Exists

In Git tools:

Branch
     ↓
Work


In Fabric:

Branch
     ↓
Need isolated workspace
     ↓
Work


Branch Out automates creating that isolated workspace.

The Core Difference
Traditional Git
Branch = Code Isolation


You isolate the files.

Fabric
Branch = Code Isolation

Workspace = Runtime Isolation


You often need both.

Real-World Analogy
Git / VS Code

Think of a Word document.

Proposal.docx


Create a branch:

Proposal_v2.docx


It's just another copy of the document.

Fabric

Think of an entire restaurant.

Kitchen
Staff
Menu
Inventory
Customers


Creating a branch isn't enough.

If you want to experiment safely, you may need:

Restaurant A (main)

Restaurant B (feature)


That's why Fabric introduced Branch Out.

One-Sentence Summary

In most Git tools, a branch is just another version of files; in Fabric, a branch often needs its own workspace because the workspace is a live analytics environment, not just a collection of files.
